<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  **Why this app permits cleartext at all.**

  An operator's administration surface is plain HTTP, and
  `infra-client-requirements.md` §8.2 is deliberate about why that is not a
  gap: administration is out of band, "reached the way any other server is
  reached", and "this document set does not specify the channel". The
  operator chose where their instance runs and how they reach it — a local
  network, a tunnel, a private link — and the protocol does not reach into
  that choice.

  **Nothing on that channel carries a secret.** The enrolment token is used
  as a MAC key and never sent; the run and the two records of §4.4 are
  signed objects, so their integrity does not rest on the transport. An
  eavesdropper learns the node's state, which §8 discloses to its operator
  anyway.

  **What it does cost**, said plainly: someone able to modify that traffic
  can modify the page the operator reads. They cannot forge a signed
  object, so they cannot change what the node holds — but they can lie
  about it, and an operator acting on a tampered page is acting on
  something they did not see. The channel is the operator's to protect, and
  this app cannot do it for them.

  **Why it is not scoped narrower.** An instance lives at whatever address
  its operator gave it, so there is no domain to name here: a list of hosts
  would be a list nobody could write in advance.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
